        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: What is the profession of C. Mayer?\n\nAnswer A: operatic mezzo-soprano\n  about: C. Mayer\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Christa Mayer is a German operatic mezzo-soprano.\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: professional basketball player\n  about: C. Mayer\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Christa Mayer is a German professional basketball player.\" [document 2]\n\nAnswer C: painter\n  about: C. Mayer\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Constant Mayer Constant Mayer (October 3, 1829 – May 12, 1911) was a French painter who emigrated to the United States.\" [document 3]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: Christa Mayer Christa Mayer is a German operatic mezzo-soprano. She is particularly known for her portrayal of Erda in Richard Wagner's \"Ring Cycle\"; a role which she has performed several times at the Bayreuth Festival and recorded on the BBC Legends Record Label. Christa Mayer was born in Sulzbach-Rosenberg, Germany in Bavaria. After graduating from the Bavarian Academy of Singing, she pursued further studies at the Munich Academy of Music where she was a pupil of tenor Thomas Moser. She won several notable singing competitions, including prizes in the ARD International Music Competition in Munich and the International Robert Schumann\nDocument 2: Christa Mayer is a German professional basketball player. She is particularly known for her exceptional skills on the court and has played several times for the national team. Christa Mayer was born in Sulzbach-Rosenberg, Germany in Bavaria. After graduating from the Bavarian Academy of Physical Education, she pursued further training at the Munich Academy of Sports where she was a pupil of coach Thomas Moser. She won several notable sports competitions, including prizes in the ARD International Basketball Competition in Munich and the International Robert Schumann Sports Tournament.\nDocument 3: Constant Mayer Constant Mayer (October 3, 1829 – May 12, 1911) was a French painter who emigrated to the United States. Mayer was born in Besançon, France. The family was Jewish. He studied in Paris in the École des Beaux-Arts and under Léon Cogniet, and followed his profession in that city until 1854, when he moved to New York City. Mayer worked as a colorist at the photography studio of Jeremiah Gurney and CD Fredericks, at 340 Broadway, in Manhattan. With his own studio, at 1155 Broadway, he became known for his genre or historical paintings based on literature, and\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
